  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", gives the same answer on two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. Reply A is the string: \"Here is a draft:\\n```json\\n{\\\"draft\\\": true} some prose after the object\\n```\\nFinal answer:\\n{\\\"final\\\": 42}\". Reply B is the string: \"Final answer:\\n{\\\"final\\\": 42}\". In Reply A, the single code fence holds one JSON object, {\\\"draft\\\": true}, followed by prose inside the fence; the rule therefore requires the check to score that fenced object, returning {\\\"draft\\\": true}. In Reply B there is no fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, {\\\"final\\\": 42}. The check, however, treats the entire fence content as one candidate; because the fence content is not valid JSON as a whole, it is skipped, and the check falls back to the top-level pool, where the last top-level object in Reply A is {\\\"final\\\": 42}, the same as in Reply B. Thus the check returns {\\\"final\\\": 42} for both, collapsing the difference. I would be wrong if the rule's phrase \"when any fence holds one\" is read to require the fence content to be exactly one scoreable JSON object with no other text, in which case Reply A's fence does not hold one and the fallback to the last top-level object is correct; or if the check's top-level extraction excluded fenced regions, which it does not."
}